-- allium: 3
--
-- Poodl — the daily word
--
-- Scope
--   One word a day, the same for everyone who plays it: which word a day
--   gets, the one game a player may play on it, what is kept of that game
--   while another mode is played, and what is remembered across days.
--
-- Includes
--   - The calendar: how days are numbered from an epoch, and where the day
--     turns — local midnight in one named zone, wherever the player is.
--   - The day's answer: taken from a schedule supplied as data, in a fixed
--     order, so that every device carrying the same schedule agrees.
--   - The one game a day: starting it, returning to it, keeping it aside
--     while another mode is played, and replacing it when a later day's game
--     starts.
--   - Daily statistics: days played, days won, the streak of consecutive
--     days won, the longest such streak, the distribution of winning guess
--     counts, and the reset that clears them together with the primary
--     block.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Playing the game (game.allium). A daily game is an ordinary Game with
--     mode = daily: letters, scoring, hard mode, winning and losing are all
--     game.allium's, and this module supplies an answer by emitting
--     AnswerDrawn exactly as statistics.allium and sharing.allium do.
--   - What the schedule must satisfy as data (words.allium). This module
--     reads it through daily_schedule() and states nothing about its shape.
--   - The daily heading on a shared grid (sharing.allium).
--   - Random and endless statistics, and the answer pool (statistics.allium).
--   - How the zone's rules are obtained, how the day is computed, and how
--     any of this is stored.
--
-- Dependencies
--   words.allium — the Word entity, the answer list and the schedule.
--   game.allium — Player, Game, NewGameRequested, AnswerDrawn and RetireGame.
--   statistics.allium — PlayerResetsStatistics, so that one reset clears
--     both blocks.

use "./words.allium" as words
use "./game.allium" as game
use "./statistics.allium" as statistics

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Which day it is. Days are numbered, not dated, because a number is what the
-- schedule is indexed by and what a shared grid carries; the date behind day
-- one is stated once, in config, and read by nothing else.
contract DailyCalendar {
    day_of: (moment: Timestamp) -> Integer
    day_start: (day: Integer) -> Timestamp

    @invariant DaysAreCountedFromTheEpoch
        -- day_of is 1 for every moment on config.epoch_date, reckoned as a
        -- calendar date in config.calendar_zone, 2 for every moment on the
        -- following date, and so on without gaps. A moment before the epoch
        -- is treated as day 1 as well: the schedule began then, and a clock
        -- that says earlier is a clock that is wrong, not a day that is
        -- missing.

    @invariant TheDayTurnsAtLocalMidnight
        -- The day advances exactly when the calendar date in
        -- config.calendar_zone advances, at that zone's own midnight, and at
        -- no other moment. Where the player is, and what their device's zone
        -- is, change nothing: two players anywhere on Earth are on the same
        -- day at the same instant.

    @invariant DayStartIsTheBoundary
        -- day_start(d) is the earliest moment whose day_of is d, for every
        -- day from two onwards. Day one is the exception
        -- DaysAreCountedFromTheEpoch makes: it folds every earlier moment
        -- into itself, so day one has no earliest moment to name, and
        -- day_start(1) is the epoch date's own local midnight — where the
        -- day would have begun had the clamp not reached back past it.
        -- Either way it is what a surface shows as the moment the next word
        -- arrives.

    @invariant Deterministic
        -- The same moment yields the same day on every device and every
        -- build carrying the same configuration. Nothing here consults the
        -- player, their settings or their preferences.

    @guidance
        -- Local midnight in config.calendar_zone is not a fixed offset from
        -- UTC. The zone observes daylight saving time, so a day is
        -- occasionally twenty-three or twenty-five hours long, and adding
        -- twenty-four hours to find the next boundary is wrong twice a year.
        -- The zone's own rules, as the platform's time zone data states them,
        -- are the intended source; nothing in this module restates them.
        --
        -- day_of is exact; a surface reading it is not. These invariants
        -- constrain a function of a moment, not how often anything asks for
        -- one. A view already on screen crosses the boundary when it next
        -- looks, so it may go on naming the earlier day for a short while
        -- after midnight, by an interval the implementation chooses and
        -- states rather than one stated here. Nothing rule-bearing rides on
        -- that lag: every request reads the clock as it is made, so a player
        -- who chooses Daily a second after midnight gets the new day's word
        -- whatever the last look said.
}

-- Which word a day gets. The schedule is data, supplied on words.allium's
-- terms; this contract says only how a day indexes into it.
contract DailyAnswer {
    daily_answer: (day: Integer) -> words/Word

    @invariant TheDayPlaysItsEntry
        -- The word for day n is entry ((n - 1) mod count) + 1 of
        -- words/daily_schedule(), where count is the number of entries: day
        -- 1 plays the first entry, day count plays the last, and day
        -- count + 1 starts again from the first. Like GuessScoring's two
        -- passes, this is the definition and not a suggestion.

    @invariant EveryDailyAnswerIsAnAnswerWord
        -- daily_answer(n) is in words/answer_words() for every n, because
        -- the schedule is drawn from that list and from nowhere else.

    @invariant SameDaySameWordEverywhere
        -- For a given day, every device and every build carrying the same
        -- schedule produces the same word. The word depends on the day and
        -- the schedule, never on the player, the device or the clock beyond
        -- the day it named.

    @invariant NoRepeatWithinOnePass
        -- Two days in the same pass through the schedule never share a word,
        -- because no word is scheduled twice; a word comes round again only
        -- once every entry has had its day.

    @guidance
        -- The wrap is what happens instead of nothing. Appending to the
        -- schedule before it runs out is the intended maintenance, and within
        -- the first pass an append changes no day already played or already
        -- scheduled: a day that would have wrapped plays the appended word
        -- instead, which is the point. After a wrap the arithmetic is less
        -- kind — each append shifts every later day by one — and that is
        -- stated here rather than hidden, so that whoever extends a schedule
        -- that has already wrapped knows what moves.
        --
        -- The day a game is for is the day it was started on.
        -- StartTodaysDailyGame reads day_of(now), and BeginGame stamps
        -- started_at with now in the same chain, so day_of(started_at) is
        -- the day the word was chosen for. An implementation decides the day
        -- once and stamps it, rather than reading the clock a second time
        -- after the emission.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The daily record. One block, declared as a default below rather than
-- created by any rule, and separate from statistics.allium's: a streak of
-- days is a different quantity from a streak of games, and a run of random
-- games neither builds nor breaks it.
entity DailyStatistics {
    days_played: Integer
    days_won: Integer

    -- The run of consecutive days won, as it stood when the last daily game
    -- was finished by play. A day that has since passed unwon has ended it;
    -- the panel reads last_won_day to tell, because a rule fires only when a
    -- game ends and a missed day ends no game.
    current_streak: Integer
    max_streak: Integer

    -- The day of the most recent win. Null until the first.
    last_won_day: Integer?

    -- Relationships
    buckets: DailyDistributionBucket with block = this

    -- Derived
    losses: days_played - days_won
    win_percentage: if days_played = 0: 0.0 else: days_won * 100.0 / days_played

    invariant WinsFallWithinDaysPlayed {
        days_won >= 0 and days_won <= days_played
    }

    invariant CurrentStreakNeverExceedsMaximum {
        current_streak >= 0 and current_streak <= max_streak
    }

    -- The distribution counts wins and only wins, so it accounts for every
    -- win and nothing else. A loss moves days_played and the streak, never a
    -- bucket.
    invariant DistributionAccountsForEveryWin {
        sum(buckets, b => b.win_count) = days_won
    }

    invariant StreakCannotExceedWins {
        current_streak <= days_won
    }

    invariant AStreakImpliesAWin {
        current_streak > 0 implies last_won_day != null
    }
}

-- One bucket per attempt count: how many days were won in that many guesses.
-- statistics.allium's bucket cannot be reused: its back reference is typed to
-- that module's block.
entity DailyDistributionBucket {
    block: DailyStatistics
    guesses_used: Integer
    win_count: Integer

    invariant BucketIsAnAttemptNumber {
        guesses_used >= 1 and guesses_used <= game/config.max_attempts
    }

    invariant WinCountIsNeverNegative {
        win_count >= 0
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The calendar date of day 1, as a date in calendar_zone. Every day is
    -- numbered from here, and the schedule is indexed by that number, so
    -- moving this renumbers every day and re-pairs every word: it is written
    -- once, before the mode ships, and then left alone.
    epoch_date: String = "2026-09-01"

    -- The zone whose local midnight turns the day, named as the IANA time
    -- zone database names it: the Pacific coast of North America, which
    -- observes daylight saving time. See DailyCalendar's guidance.
    calendar_zone: String = "America/Los_Angeles"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Not named "daily": a bound name resolves before an enum literal, and this
-- module compares modes against daily on every rule.
default DailyStatistics daily_block = {
    days_played: 0,
    days_won: 0,
    current_streak: 0,
    max_streak: 0
}

default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_one = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 1, win_count: 0 }
default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_two = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 2, win_count: 0 }
default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_three = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 3, win_count: 0 }
default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_four = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 4, win_count: 0 }
default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_five = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 5, win_count: 0 }
default DailyDistributionBucket daily_bucket_six = { block: daily_block, guesses_used: 6, win_count: 0 }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Choosing Daily ---------------------------------------------------------

-- Three rules answer the one request, and which fires depends on what is
-- kept: nothing, or a game from an earlier day; today's game, off the board;
-- today's game, on the board. The mode guard keeps all three apart from
-- game.allium's practice rule and statistics.allium's pooled draw, which
-- answer the same trigger for the other modes.

-- No game for today exists yet, so today's word is drawn. game.allium takes
-- it from here: BeginGame retires whatever is on the board and creates the
-- game, and KeepTodaysDailyGame below then keeps it.
rule StartTodaysDailyGame {
    when: game/NewGameRequested(player, mode)

    requires: mode = daily

    let today = day_of(now)
    let kept = player.daily_game

    requires: not exists kept or day_of(kept.started_at) != today

    ensures: game/AnswerDrawn(player: player, mode: daily, answer: daily_answer(today))

    @guidance
        -- A kept game from an earlier day is not removed here. It goes when
        -- the new game is created, in KeepTodaysDailyGame, so that the two
        -- cannot come apart: no daily game is ever removed without another
        -- taking its place. Nothing about the old game is recorded, finished
        -- or not: an unfinished day is a day not won, which the streak
        -- already reads as such (see RecordDailyWin).
        --
        -- The mode is a literal, as in ProvidePracticeAnswer: the request
        -- carries a StartableMode and a game carries a GameMode, and the
        -- requires clause has already fixed which one this is.
}

-- Today's game exists and is off the board — set aside while another mode
-- was played — so it comes back. This is the one way a game becomes current
-- without being created, which is why it does for itself what BeginGame and
-- MakeNewGameCurrent do between them: retire the outgoing game, take the
-- board, dismiss the welcome screen, remember the mode.
rule ReturnToTodaysDailyGame {
    when: game/NewGameRequested(player, mode)

    requires: mode = daily

    let today = day_of(now)
    let kept = player.daily_game

    requires: exists kept
    requires: day_of(kept.started_at) = today
    requires: not kept.is_current

    let outgoing = player.current_game

    ensures:
        if exists outgoing:
            game/RetireGame(game: outgoing)
        player.current_game = kept
        player.awaiting_welcome = false
        player.last_mode = daily

    @guidance
        -- The outgoing game is retired on game.allium's ordinary terms, so
        -- coming back to Daily part way through a random game costs that
        -- game exactly what starting any other game would: a loss if it had
        -- a guess in it, nothing if it had none. The kept game comes back
        -- exactly as it was left — its guesses, its typed letters, and its
        -- conclusion if it had one.
}

-- Today's game is already on the board. There is no second game to start and
-- nothing to retire; only the welcome screen, if that is what the player was
-- looking at, is dismissed — the same as continuing. Without this rule a
-- player who reloaded part way through today's game and chose Daily rather
-- than Continue would be left on the welcome screen.
rule StayOnTodaysDailyGame {
    when: game/NewGameRequested(player, mode)

    requires: mode = daily

    let today = day_of(now)
    let kept = player.daily_game

    requires: exists kept
    requires: day_of(kept.started_at) = today
    requires: kept.is_current

    ensures: player.awaiting_welcome = false
}

-- Keeping the game --------------------------------------------------------

-- The new daily game becomes the kept one, and the one kept until now goes.
-- This is the single place a daily game is removed: game.allium's retirement
-- rules leave a daily game alone, so leaving the board never removes one, and
-- only the next daily game does.
rule KeepTodaysDailyGame {
    when: g: game/Game.created

    requires: g.mode = daily

    let previous = g.player.daily_game

    ensures:
        g.player.daily_game = g
        if exists previous:
            not exists previous

    @guidance
        -- previous is bound before the ensures block, so it is the game kept
        -- until now and not the one being kept from now on. When that game
        -- was still on the board, BeginGame has already retired it — to no
        -- effect, by the guards on game.allium's retirement rules — and
        -- MakeNewGameCurrent has put the new game there; the removal here
        -- completes what those two began.
}

-- Recording a day --------------------------------------------------------

-- A win: one more day played, one more won, a longer streak if yesterday was
-- won too and a streak of one otherwise, and a mark in the bucket for however
-- many guesses it took.
rule RecordDailyWin {
    when: g: game/Game.status transitions_to won

    requires: g.mode = daily

    let day = day_of(g.started_at)
    let streak = if daily_block.last_won_day = day - 1: daily_block.current_streak + 1 else: 1
    let bucket = DailyDistributionBucket{block: daily_block, guesses_used: g.attempts_used}

    requires: exists bucket

    ensures:
        daily_block.days_played = daily_block.days_played + 1
        daily_block.days_won = daily_block.days_won + 1
        daily_block.current_streak = streak
        daily_block.last_won_day = day
        bucket.win_count = bucket.win_count + 1
        if streak > daily_block.max_streak:
            daily_block.max_streak = streak

    @guidance
        -- A streak is consecutive days won, not consecutive games: a day
        -- skipped, or started and never finished, ends it as surely as a
        -- loss does, because the next win finds last_won_day further back
        -- than yesterday. A null last_won_day compares false, so the first
        -- win ever starts a streak of one without a case of its own.
        --
        -- The day is the game's, not the clock's: a game finished after
        -- midnight counts for the day it was started on, which is the day
        -- whose word it was given. The streak is bound before the ensures
        -- block for the reason RecordWin gives.
}

-- A loss: the day counts, the streak goes, and the distribution is untouched.
rule RecordDailyLoss {
    when: g: game/Game.status transitions_to lost

    requires: g.mode = daily

    ensures:
        daily_block.days_played = daily_block.days_played + 1
        daily_block.current_streak = 0
}

-- Starting over ----------------------------------------------------------

-- One action clears one record of play. statistics.allium's reset clears the
-- numbers and the pool; this block goes with them, on the same action, and
-- the confirmation there names it. The kept game is not touched: it is a
-- game on the board or set aside, not a statistic, exactly as the primary
-- reset leaves the current game alone.
rule ResetDailyStatisticsToo {
    when: statistics/PlayerResetsStatistics(player)

    ensures:
        daily_block.days_played = 0
        daily_block.days_won = 0
        daily_block.current_streak = 0
        daily_block.max_streak = 0
        daily_block.last_won_day = null
        for b in daily_block.buckets:
            b.win_count = 0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A daily game plays the word its day was given, and no other. Stated over
-- stored state — the game's start, not the clock — so that it holds of a
-- game finished after midnight as much as of one finished before.
invariant TheDailyGamePlaysItsDaysWord {
    for g in game/Games:
        g.mode = daily implies g.answer = daily_answer(day_of(g.started_at))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What Daily shows that the board does not: which day it is, whether today's
-- game exists and how it stands, and when the next word arrives. Choosing
-- Daily is not provided here: it is game.allium's NewGameRequested with mode
-- daily, offered by Welcome, GameNavigation and GameBoard alongside the other
-- modes, and this module answers it. GameConclusion is not among them: a
-- finished daily game offers no mode at all, only the way back to the welcome
-- screen where they are chosen. No related clause names those surfaces, for
-- the reason sharing.allium gives; the adjacency is in the guarantees.
surface TodaysGame {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    let today = day_of(now)
    let kept = viewer.daily_game

    -- The kept game's own day, exposed beside today's so that a game from an
    -- earlier day, still under way or still on the board, is never mistaken
    -- for no game at all or for today's.
    let kept_day = if exists kept: day_of(kept.started_at) else: null
    let is_todays = kept_day = today
    let next_word_at = day_start(today + 1)

    exposes:
        today
        kept_day
        kept?.status
        kept?.is_current
        is_todays
        next_word_at

    contracts:
        fulfils DailyCalendar
        fulfils DailyAnswer

    @guarantee OneWordADayForEveryone
        -- Every player on the same build plays the same word on the same
        -- day. The word depends on the day and the schedule, on the terms
        -- DailyAnswer states, and on nothing about the player; the day
        -- depends on the moment and config.calendar_zone, on the terms
        -- DailyCalendar states, and on nothing about where they are. A
        -- player in another zone gets the new word at the same instant, not
        -- at their own midnight.

    @guarantee OneGameADay
        -- Today's word can be played once. Choosing Daily when today's game
        -- exists brings that game back in whatever state it was left — under
        -- way with its guesses and typed letters, or finished with its
        -- conclusion showing — and never starts another game on the same
        -- word. A finished game comes back onto game.allium's
        -- GameConclusion, exactly as a finished game resumed by Continue
        -- does.

    @guarantee TheDayIsPerceivable
        -- Which day's word is on the board is readable as text — the day
        -- number — and so is whether today's game has been played and how
        -- it ended. Neither is signalled only by which control looks
        -- selected or by a colour.

    @guarantee TheNextWordIsAnnouncedInAdvance
        -- When the next word arrives is available as text from the moment
        -- today's game is over. Once the date in config.calendar_zone has
        -- moved on, a game from the earlier day still on the board is said
        -- to be that day's, and the new word is said to be available, rather
        -- than the old game being left looking like today's. Available means
        -- available where the player is looking: while today's finished game
        -- waits off the board, the surfaces that offer Daily say the time
        -- too, because the game that would otherwise carry it is not on
        -- screen and coming back to it costs the game that is.

    @guarantee LeavingDailyDoesNotSpendTheDay
        -- Starting a game in another mode, or opening a custom link, sets
        -- today's game aside with its guesses and its typed letters. It is
        -- not abandoned, nothing is counted, and choosing Daily brings it
        -- back. The other modes' games pay their ordinary price when Daily
        -- is chosen over them: a random game with a guess in it counts as a
        -- loss, on the terms game.allium's GameNavigation states.

    @guarantee TodaysGameSurvivesReload
        -- Today's game persists between sessions on the same device and
        -- browser, on the board or set aside, exactly as game.allium's
        -- InProgressGameSurvivesReload promises for the game on the board.
        -- Reopening Poodl with Daily as the remembered mode meets the
        -- welcome screen, unless it has been turned off, and Continue
        -- returns to today's game rather than drawing a new one.

    @guarantee ANewDayReplacesTheOldGame
        -- Once the date in config.calendar_zone has moved on, choosing Daily
        -- starts the new day's game, and the earlier day's game goes,
        -- finished or not. An unfinished earlier day is recorded nowhere:
        -- the streak, which counts consecutive days won, already reads it as
        -- a day not won. Where the choice would discard a game still under
        -- way, it says so before it is taken, as
        -- StartingAGameEndsTheOneUnderWay does for the other modes.

    @guarantee ThereIsNoNewGameInDaily
        -- No control offers a second daily game. Where the other modes offer
        -- a new game of the same kind, Daily offers the time the next word
        -- arrives and the way back to the welcome screen, where another mode
        -- is chosen; asking for Daily while today's game is on the board
        -- leaves it exactly where it is.

    @guarantee TheWordIsAsHiddenAsAnyOther
        -- Today's answer is a function of the date and the bundled schedule,
        -- so anyone who reads the bundle can find tomorrow's word. That is
        -- the trade custom links already make — there is no server to keep a
        -- word on — and it is obfuscation, not security, on the terms
        -- docs/decisions/0005-obfuscation-not-security.md states. Nothing on
        -- this surface reveals the answer, or any letter of it, before the
        -- game ends: AnswerIsNeverExposedWhileInProgress holds for a daily
        -- game as for any other.
}

-- The daily record, shown with statistics.allium's panel. It has no control
-- of its own: the reset that clears it is that panel's, and one action clears
-- both blocks.
surface DailyStatisticsPanel {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    let stats = daily_block
    let today = day_of(now)
    let live_streak = if stats.last_won_day >= today - 1: stats.current_streak else: 0

    exposes:
        stats.days_played
        stats.days_won
        stats.losses
        stats.win_percentage
        live_streak
        stats.max_streak
        for bucket in stats.buckets:
            bucket.guesses_used
            bucket.win_count

    @guarantee OnlyDailyGamesAreCountedHere
        -- Random, endless, practice and custom games never move any number
        -- on this panel, and daily games never move statistics.allium's. One
        -- game moves one block.

    @guarantee AStreakIsConsecutiveDays
        -- The streak counts days won in a row, not games. It is shown as it
        -- stands today: a day that has passed unwon ends it, and the panel
        -- shows zero from the next day on, before the player plays again,
        -- rather than the number the last win left behind. The longest
        -- streak is unaffected.

    @guarantee DistributionIsReadableWithoutSeeingIt
        -- Each bucket's attempt number and count are available as text, so
        -- the distribution can be read by assistive technology rather than
        -- inferred from the length of a bar.

    @guarantee DailyStatisticsPersistBetweenSessions
        -- Every number on this panel persists between sessions on the same
        -- device and browser.

    @guarantee ResetTogetherWithTheRest
        -- statistics.allium's reset clears this block on the same action,
        -- and its confirmation says so. There is no separate reset for the
        -- daily record, and no way to clear one block and keep the other.

    @guarantee ShownBesideThePrimaryBlock
        -- This record is shown with statistics.allium's StatisticsPanel,
        -- reachable from wherever that panel is, and labelled as the daily
        -- record so that its days are never read as that panel's games.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Today's word is drawn from the schedule and the pool knows nothing of it,
-- so the same word can come up in a random or endless game on the same day.
-- Whether the daily answer should be recorded as used, or withheld from the
-- pool until its day has passed, is not decided.
open question "Pool overlap - should the day's word be withheld from the random and endless pool?"
